From: SamuriHL <samurihl@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 17:27:59 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] disc time: one process-lifetime disc clock on the timeline

Phase 5 step 5.D sub-steps 1-3 (design sec. 18.6-18.8), with the
code review's fixes.

1. The disc clock (C) and its natives.

A static clock in util/time.c (not in the BLURAY object, so lingering
xlet threads never read freed memory), guarded by its own small mutex
(never bd->mutex). Disc ns run from the first use in the process and
never go back, across discs too. bd_disc_clock_start() (a playback
open only) records the disc ns and wall time at the disc's start, for
BD-J millis; the wall ms recorded is max(wall now, the millis the clock
returns now), so millis never go back across discs when the wall clock
was stepped back between them. bd_disc_clock_set(running, at_mono_ns)
freezes at now or runs from an instant that may be in the future
(readers apply max(0, now - anchor), so the clock stays frozen until
that instant and starts exactly there); a past run instant runs at now.

Every write also records a segment {from_mono, anchor_mono,
anchor_disc, running} in a ring of the last 8. disc_time_at(t) uses the
latest segment in force at t (from_mono = the write's instant <= t), so
an instant before the latest write (patch 17's start conversion, up to
~100 ms after T0) is not moved by a freeze or run written in between,
and a pending future RUN keeps it frozen until its anchor. Instants
older than the ring use the oldest segment, counted (DISCDIAG TEMP).

The graphics controller's eight bd_get_scr() reads use the disc clock
in 90 kHz units (bd_get_scr() when disc time is off).

Static natives Libbluray.discTimeNanosN(), discTimeAtN(long monoNs) and
discTimeMillisN() read the clock with no BLURAY pointer.

BLURAY_PLAYER_SETTING_DISC_TIME_OFF (process-wide, before playback):
the clock never freezes, the graphics controller keeps bd_get_scr(),
and libbluray sets the Java property bluray.disctime.off on every BD-J
start before Libbluray.init.

Rule: the clock mutex guards a copy of a few words; nothing logs, calls
into Java or takes another lock under it.

DISCDIAG TEMP: every clock write is logged.

2. BD-J helpers, DiscTimer and the source changes.

org.videolan.DiscTime: currentTimeMillis() (no synchronized: the
native's mutex is the barrier), nanoTime(), sleep(J)/(JI),
waitOn(Object,J)/(Object,J,I) and joinOn(Thread,J)/(Thread,J,I) with
the JDK 8u462 checks and semantics: IllegalArgumentException (and the
NullPointerException of a null receiver) before any monitor is touched;
sleep(0), wait(0,0) and join(0) passed through; wait(0,n>0) a 1 ms wait;
the JDK's own IllegalMonitorStateException and InterruptedException.
Waits run on wall time towards the disc deadline: a wall wait that ends
with disc time short waits again, in slices of min(remaining, 50) ms
only while frozen; a remainder rounds up (never a wait(0)); deadlines
saturate. waitOn returns at once on an early wake; a notify coinciding
with a wall timeout during a freeze is lost until the disc deadline
(inherent to wait(J)), counted.

org.videolan.DiscTimer / DiscTimerTask: java.util.Timer / TimerTask
(OpenJDK 8 semantics) on disc ms, not final, the timer thread created
in the caller's thread group. The queue loop waits on its own queue with
a wall timeout of the disc time left and re-checks, in slices of at most
50 ms only while frozen.

In source, each taking the off flag (bluray.disctime.off, re-read at
every Libbluray.init): TVTimerImpl on DiscTimer and disc time for
absolute specs; SystemTimeBase is disc nanoseconds (no lazy wall start);
ImageFrameAccurateAnimation's sleep(40) -> DiscTime.sleep; the
org.dvb.event UserEvent `when` from DiscTime.currentTimeMillis; patch
17's start conversion uses Libbluray.discTimeAt(startNs). Off: every
helper is the JDK call it replaces and each source change is today's
code.

Left on wall time: BDRootWindow's 40 ms flush timer, the xlet stop and
5 s disposal waits, patch 17's poll timer, pendingSince and 15 s limit,
and the AWT key/mouse event `when` (java.awt.BDJHelper): JDK 8's
type-ahead markers compare it against wall-stamped invokeLater events,
so a disc-stamped key after a freeze would go to the old focus owner.

DISCDIAG TEMP: per-kind counters (sleep frozen waits and slices, waitOn
early wakes / timeouts / re-waits / timeouts after a freeze, joinOn,
DiscTimer firings and frozen re-checks), logged at most once per 10 s,
at xlet shutdown and before the next disc's start (then reset).

3. The disc class patcher and one define path.

org.videolan.DiscClassPatcher (ASM 5.0.4: ClassReader EXPAND_FRAMES,
ClassWriter flags 0, debug info kept), built in the BDJXletContext
constructor for BDJClassLoader and in DVBClassLoader's URLClassLoader:
- invokestatic java/lang/System.currentTimeMillis()J / nanoTime()J ->
  DiscTime;
- invokestatic sleep(J)V / (JI)V -> DiscTime.sleep, and
  invokevirtual/special/interface wait(J)V / (JI)V -> DiscTime.waitOn,
  join(J)V / (JI)V -> DiscTime.joinOn (static, the receiver is the first
  argument: same operand stack), only when the call resolves to the JDK
  method: no disc class on the owner's superclass chain declares the
  name and descriptor, and the chain reaches java/lang/Thread (sleep,
  join). The chain is read from the loader's own class bytes, never by
  loading; the first class outside them is resolved through the system
  class loader (JDK / libbluray only; DVBClassLoader walks on through a
  disc parent's bytes). Anything unresolved is left unchanged.
- only for classes whose constant pool names java/util/Timer* (pre-scan
  of every Utf8 entry, descriptors and signatures included):
  RemappingClassAdapter java/util/Timer -> org/videolan/DiscTimer,
  java/util/TimerTask -> org/videolan/DiscTimerTask (subclasses and
  their super.<init> calls included). Other classes never pass through
  the remapper's LocalVariablesSorter.
- a class with no rewrite is defined from its original bytes.

org.videolan.DiscClassDefiner: one define path for every disc class
(patched, unchanged, stripped): the 5-argument defineClass with a
CodeSource of JarURLConnection.getJarFileURL() and the entry's code
signers read after the entry is read to EOF, definePackage from the jar
manifest with URLClassLoader's sealing check; directory AppCaches: the
classpath directory URL, no signers, no manifest. The jars are read
through the definer's own JarFiles (closed with the loader), never the
JarURLConnection cache, which would outlive the loader and serve a
stale jar to a later disc with the same jar path. A class with broken
debug info is patched with it dropped; a ClassFormatError from
defineClass retries once stripped (BDJClassFileTransformer.strip),
patched again (DVBClassLoader gains this fallback). A class the patcher
cannot parse is defined unpatched with an ERROR, except one that names
the Timer types, which fails with an ERROR naming it.

Off (bluray.disctime.off): no patcher and no definer; BDJClassLoader's
findClass, its strip path and the Evangelion ReplaceMethodPatcher are
today's, and DVBClassLoader is today's. On: the Evangelion patcher is
not installed (DiscTime.currentTimeMillis goes through the native's
mutex, the barrier it needed).

DISCDIAG TEMP: per loader, a line per jar at its first class (signed,
manifest, sealed) and a summary (classes, patched, timer_remapped,
stripped, debug_dropped, signed, counts by rewrite kind, calls kept as
overrides / unresolved, "java.util.Timer" strings, failures by class)
after the xlet class load, at xlet shutdown and at most once per 10 s.


